  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe center of an upper level ridge shifts from eastern New Mexico \ntoward the Desert Southwest late this week and into the weekend. \nSubsidence below the ridge generally keeps hot and dry conditions in \nthe forecast through the remainder of the workweek. However, \ngradually decreasing heights allow for slightly cooler conditions \ncompared to earlier in the week. Temperatures Thursday and Friday \nare forecast to top out in the upper 90s and triple digits, with \nFriday being the hotter of the two days. The hottest conditions are \nexpected near and along the Rio Grande, where highs both afternoons \nwill range between 105-112 degrees. As the ridge retrogrades, rain \nchances return to portions of our area. Friday evening, isolated \nshowers/storms may develop over SE NM. Saturday, a trough currently \nover the northeastern US dives south, sending a cold front toward \nour region. The front will have little effect on temperatures, \nmainly just shaving off a few degrees from the previous days, but \nlift from this feature brings additional rain chances into the \nforecast. Rain chances remain low (10-20%) across the region between \nSaturday and Sunday. Warmer and drier conditions return at the start \nof next week. Overnight lows largely cool into the 70s each night.",
